[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Memory Leaks and Leaked Objects: Debugging with Instruments (Leaks, Allocations)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Memory management in iOS is handled via Automatic Reference Counting (ARC). However, ARC is not infallible. Strong reference cycles, commonly known as memory leaks, occur when two or more objects hold strong references to each other, preventing ARC from deallocating them and leading to increased memory footprints and eventual app crashes."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Retain Cycles in Swift"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A retain cycle frequently happens within closures or delegate patterns when self is captured strongly. Consider the following example of a class holding a closure that captures self without a capture list."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"class NetworkManager {
    var completionHandler: (() -> Void)?

    func fetchData() {
        completionHandler = {
            // Retain cycle created here because self is captured strongly
            self.processData()
        }
    }

    func processData() {
        print("Processing...")
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"To resolve this, we must introduce a capture list using weak or unowned references. This tells the runtime not to increment the reference count of the captured instance."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"class NetworkManager {
    var completionHandler: (() -> Void)?

    func fetchData() {
        completionHandler = { [weak self] in
            guard let self = self else { return }
            self.processData()
        }
    }

    func processData() {
        print("Processing...")
    }
}"},{"order":8,"type":"heading","text":"Preparing for Profiling with Xcode Instruments"},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Open your project in Xcode."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Select a physical iOS device or a simulator for profiling. Note: Leaks instrument is most accurate on physical devices."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Navigate to the top menu and select Product > Profile (or press Command + I)."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"From the Instruments template selection window, choose 'Leaks' and click Choose."},{"order":13,"type":"heading","text":"Using the Leaks Instrument"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once Instruments launches and your app starts running, perform actions in your app that you suspect might cause leaks (e.g., navigating back and forth between view controllers). The Leaks instrument polls the app's memory periodically to identify leaked memory blocks."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Click the red Record button in the top left of Instruments to start tracking."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Look at the timeline view. Red downward-pointing spikes indicate detected memory leaks."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pause recording and select 'Leaks' in the detail pane dropdown to inspect specific leaked objects."},{"order":18,"type":"heading","text":"Using the Allocations Instrument for Deeper Analysis"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"While the Leaks instrument finds blocks with no active references, the Allocations instrument tracks all memory allocations over time. This helps you identify abandoned memory (objects that are technically not 'leaked' by definition, but should have been deallocated based on your app state)."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Add the Allocations instrument to your profiling session."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use the 'Mark Generation' feature at the bottom of the track view. Mark Generation 1 before performing an action, perform the action, then Mark Generation 2."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Filter by your custom View Controller or model classes in the inspector to verify if instances persist when they should be nil."},{"order":23,"type":"heading","text":"Analyzing Retain Graphs in Xcode"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"Modern Xcode versions allow you to debug memory issues directly inside the IDE using the Memory Graph Debugger without switching to Instruments for quick checks."},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Run your app in Xcode and click the Debug Memory Graph icon (overlapping circles) in the debug bar."},{"order":26,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Inspect the left navigator for purple warning icons indicating runtime memory leaks."},{"order":27,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Click on a leaked object to view the exact retain cycle graph, tracing strong references point-by-point to fix the architecture."}]
